  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1205 AM \nMST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThere is better agreement through the weekend with regards to a\npassing upper trough, with larger differences by early next\nweek.\n\nThursday and Friday...overall, quiet weather will continue\nthrough the end of the work week across southern Colorado.\nThursday look nice across the region, with upper 40s to mid 50s\nfor highs across the Plains. Overnight lows will fall into the\n20s and 30s across the Plains and teens for the San Luis Valley.\n\nOn Friday, northwesterly flow increases across the region.\nEnhanced mixing will help temperatures warm nicely into the\nupper 50s to mid 60s on the Plains, with a few areas pushing 70\ndegrees. Winds will also be increasing, especially in gap wind\nand wind prone areas along the lee slopes of the Eastern\nMountains. Gusts of 30 to 35 mph are possible in these areas.\nThis will also help keep overnight temperatures warm with mostly\n30s across the Plains, and 20s for the San Luis Valley.\n\nSaturday into Sunday...models are in better agreement with a\nquick moving upper wave moving in the northwesterly flow across\nColorado. The GFS remain less aggressive than the ECMWF, but \nthe ECMWF is less amplified than it was yesterday. Saturday \nlooks windy with the upper trough passage. Northwest winds with \ngusts 40 to 50 mph are possible across the Plains. Humidity \nvalues look to remain elevated, limiting any fire weather \nconcerns. Showers will also develop across the Central Mountains\non Saturday and continue into the evening hours. Current QPF \namounts are low, and snow amounts of less than an inch are \npossible for areas north of Cottonwood Pass. The ECMWF is still \na bit more amplified with the system, and spreads showers off \nthe higher terrain into the Pikes Peak Region Saturday evening. \nNBM guidance is in the middle, with low pops across the Palmer \nDivide. At this time, the remainder of the area looks to remain \ndry. Slightly cooler temperatures are forecast for Saturday, \nwith 50s and lower 60s across the region.\n\nThe upper wave quickly shift eastward by Sunday, with a return\nto dry conditions. Winds will also weaken, and temperatures will\nbe cooler after the trough passage. Highs will only reach into\nthe 40s for much of the Plains.\n\nMonday to Wednesday...models begin to diverge for early to mid\nnext week. Overall, it looks dry, with showers possible mainly\nalong the Continental Divide. There is some potential for the\nnext storm system by Wednesday, but timing is really up in the\nair at this time. Temperatures look to remain mild with 50s\nacross the region.",
